WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000 line:80%
It's 6.37 in the morning.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.000 line:80%
That's about how well my eyes focus when I get up.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.500 line:80%
What time do you get up?

4
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.500 line:80%
I was recently at a YouTube conference where someone did a presentation on my morning routine.

5
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.500 line:80%
Different people across the world just taking their camera through their morning,

6
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.500 line:80%
showing people what their life is like, and I thought, this is fascinating.

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.000 line:80%
I loved seeing other cultures, seeing what people did, how they lived.

8
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.500 line:80%
Just a normal day.

9
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.060 line:80%
So I decided today, I'm going to do the same. I'm going to show you my morning routine.

10
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.940 line:80%
What is my morning like? And of course, we'll turn it into an English lesson along the way.

11
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.620 line:80%
I have a baby. What do you think the first thing I do every morning is?

12
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:53.140 line:80%
The first thing is always Nurse Sawyer. He's a little too distracted by the camera to nurse right now.

13
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:59.460 line:80%
Nurse. This word has several different meanings. You can nurse a baby, you can nurse a sick

14
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:05.900 line:80%
person, you can nurse a cold, you can even nurse a beer. We'll go over all of these meanings.

15
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.100 line:80%
First, I want to say the verb breastfeed can be used interchangeably with this term, and

16
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.940 line:80%
it doesn't mean anything else, so that can be another option. The first thing I do every

17
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.020 line:80%
morning is breastfeed my baby. Notice that I'm not saying the T here. That's common.

18
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.700 line:80%
When the T comes between two consonants, we often drop it.

19
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:23.760 line:80%
Breastfeed.

20
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.660 line:80%
Straight from the S sound into the F sound.

21
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:27.660 line:80%
Breastfeed.

22
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.740 line:80%
Now let's talk about all those different meanings.

23
00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.640 line:80%
If we use this word as a noun, it's somebody who's been trained as a nurse.

24
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.880 line:80%
To care for the sick, it's an occupation.

25
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.180 line:80%
My aunt is a surgical nurse.

26
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.300 line:80%
As a verb, like I used it, it can mean to breastfeed, to feed a baby.

27
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.080 line:80%
It can also be used to describe caring for someone who's sick.

28
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.240 line:80%
She nursed him back to health.

29
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.360 line:80%
It can be used to describe taking care of an ailment.

30
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.880 line:80%
I've been nursing a cold for two weeks.

31
00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.640 line:80%
That means I'm taking throat lozenges for it, drinking lots of tea, trying to get better.

32
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.300 line:80%
We also use it to talk about a drink, usually an alcoholic one, that we're drinking very

33
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:09.040 line:80%
slowly, just taking a sip every once in a while.

34
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:10.820 line:80%
Rachel, can I get you a drink?

35
00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.680 line:80%
No thanks, I'm still nursing this beer.

36
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.680 line:80%
This is something new.

37
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.020 line:80%
This is different from our routine, looking at a camera.

38
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:35.708 line:80%
One of the first things I try to do every day is make the bed which I used to never do but it like one tiny thing I can do to create a little bit of

39
00:02:36.028 --> 00:02:41.388 line:80%
organization in a life that is otherwise very chaotic right now.

40
00:02:41.388 --> 00:02:46.748 line:80%
Chaotic. Chaos. Here the CH makes a hard K sound.

41
00:02:46.748 --> 00:02:50.588 line:80%
It's also common to make a CH sound like in chest, ch,

42
00:02:50.588 --> 00:02:52.988 line:80%
or an SH sound like in Chicago.

43
00:02:52.988 --> 00:02:54.108 line:80%
Shhh.

44
00:02:54.108 --> 00:03:02.828 line:80%
CH makes a K in choir, echo, chemistry, anchor, stomach, orchestra, and many others.

45
00:03:02.828 --> 00:03:07.068 line:80%
You can't tell how a CH should be pronounced just by looking at it.

46
00:03:07.068 --> 00:03:08.908 line:80%
You have to know word by word.

47
00:03:08.908 --> 00:03:12.828 line:80%
This is why spelling and pronunciation is so tricky in English.

48
00:03:12.828 --> 00:03:16.588 line:80%
Then I head downstairs to have breakfast with Stoney and David.

49
00:03:16.588 --> 00:03:19.228 line:80%
I don't feel like cereal so I'm going to make an egg.

50
00:03:19.228 --> 00:03:20.708 line:80%
Does anybody want an egg?

51
00:03:20.708 --> 00:03:22.448 line:80%
No, I don't.

52
00:03:22.448 --> 00:03:23.108 line:80%
No?

53
00:03:23.108 --> 00:03:24.108 line:80%
Okay.

54
00:03:24.108 --> 00:03:26.628 line:80%
I used the phrase, feel like.

55
00:03:26.628 --> 00:03:31.748 line:80%
You can use this interchangeably with want, except the form of the verb will change.

56
00:03:31.748 --> 00:03:33.668 line:80%
I feel like going to bed.

57
00:03:33.668 --> 00:03:35.268 line:80%
I want to go to bed.

58
00:03:35.268 --> 00:03:41.348 line:80%
So with feel like, we follow it with the ING form of a verb, feel like going.

59
00:03:41.348 --> 00:03:47.068 line:80%
With want, the next verb is in the infinitive, want to go, want to go.

60
00:03:47.068 --> 00:03:50.428 line:80%
But the meaning of the two sentences is the same.

61
00:03:50.428 --> 00:03:52.428 line:80%
This applies to the negative as well.

62
00:03:52.428 --> 00:03:54.268 line:80%
I don't feel like having cereal.

63
00:03:54.268 --> 00:03:56.668 line:80%
I don't want to have cereal.

64
00:03:56.668 --> 00:03:59.548 line:80%
But when I said it, I followed it by a noun.

65
00:03:59.548 --> 00:04:02.188 line:80%
I don't feel like cereal, so I'm going to make an egg.

66
00:04:02.188 --> 00:04:04.348 line:80%
I don't feel like cereal.

67
00:04:04.348 --> 00:04:06.268 line:80%
So nothing else changes.

68
00:04:06.268 --> 00:04:08.028 line:80%
I don't feel like cereal.

69
00:04:08.028 --> 00:04:09.788 line:80%
I don't want cereal.

70
00:04:13.068 --> 00:04:16.108 line:80%
Hey Stoney, your mouth is totally full.

71
00:04:16.108 --> 00:04:21.548 line:80%
So we can't understand you, so take your time, chew, swallow, and then say what you were trying to say.

72
00:04:28.988 --> 00:04:31.488 line:80%
Good, Daddy. It's good.

73
00:04:31.848 --> 00:04:32.948 line:80%
It's good? The toast?

74
00:04:33.248 --> 00:04:33.808 line:80%
Yes.

75
00:04:34.088 --> 00:04:35.968 line:80%
Well, it looks like it's really good.

76
00:04:35.968 --> 00:04:40.668 line:80%
The most common breakfast in the U.S. is probably cereal with milk.

77
00:04:41.028 --> 00:04:42.548 line:80%
That's what David and Stoney had.

78
00:04:42.948 --> 00:04:44.608 line:80%
What do you usually have for breakfast?

79
00:04:44.608 --> 00:04:47.808 line:80%
Post it to Instagram, tag me, at Rachel's English.

80
00:04:47.808 --> 00:04:49.728 line:80%
I love to see this kind of thing.

81
00:04:49.728 --> 00:04:51.848 line:80%
What people eat in different cultures.

82
00:04:51.848 --> 00:05:03.735 line:80%
Let have breakfast together Mommy I wanna have milk Okay well how would you ask me to get you some milk Milk please Sure

83
00:05:03.735 --> 00:05:06.135 line:80%
What time are you guys getting together?

84
00:05:06.135 --> 00:05:10.135 line:80%
Here, David's talking about getting together with a friend and his kids.

85
00:05:10.135 --> 00:05:13.975 line:80%
This is a phrasal verb that means to meet, to spend time together.

86
00:05:13.975 --> 00:05:15.975 line:80%
You could also use it as a noun.

87
00:05:15.975 --> 00:05:18.375 line:80%
We're having a get together at our house this weekend.

88
00:05:18.375 --> 00:05:20.255 line:80%
Why don't you come?

89
00:05:20.255 --> 00:05:23.455 line:80%
If you use it with it, the meaning is different.

90
00:05:23.455 --> 00:05:28.095 line:80%
Get it together means to get organized or stabilized after chaos.

91
00:05:28.095 --> 00:05:31.535 line:80%
For example, if Stoney's having a tantrum, we could say,

92
00:05:31.535 --> 00:05:33.455 line:80%
Get it together, Stoney.

93
00:05:33.455 --> 00:05:38.095 line:80%
Or, at a busy time in my life lately, I missed a meeting because I totally forgot.

94
00:05:38.095 --> 00:05:44.015 line:80%
I apologized and said, I'm so sorry I forgot, I just can't get it together these days.

95
00:05:44.015 --> 00:05:46.335 line:80%
Get together is to meet.

96
00:05:46.335 --> 00:05:50.095 line:80%
get it together, is to recover from a period of chaos.

97
00:05:51.295 --> 00:05:55.695 line:80%
Mommy, where is the flashing part?

98
00:05:55.695 --> 00:05:58.815 line:80%
Oh, I put it over there.

99
00:05:58.815 --> 00:05:59.935 line:80%
There.

100
00:05:59.935 --> 00:06:01.135 line:80%
Mm-hmm.

101
00:06:01.135 --> 00:06:02.335 line:80%
Ah!

102
00:06:02.335 --> 00:06:07.135 line:80%
The flashing part. Here, Stoney's talking about a camera that had a blinking light.

103
00:06:07.135 --> 00:06:10.095 line:80%
Did you notice that Stoney can't say there yet?

104
00:06:10.095 --> 00:06:10.895 line:80%
There.

105
00:06:10.895 --> 00:06:15.775 line:80%
He can't make a TH. I've worked with him on it several times and he just can't coordinate

106
00:06:15.775 --> 00:06:20.975 line:80%
putting the tongue tip through the teeth. I know this is a huge challenge for my non-native students,

107
00:06:20.975 --> 00:06:25.615 line:80%
so I just wanted you to know it takes time. Stoney's been speaking English for over two

108
00:06:25.615 --> 00:06:30.495 line:80%
years now and he still doesn't have that sound. I'm interested to see when he picks it up.

109
00:06:31.135 --> 00:06:34.415 line:80%
And breakfast just continues with random loud noises.

110
00:06:34.415 --> 00:06:36.415 line:80%
I'll leave you, baby.

111
00:06:36.415 --> 00:06:37.415 line:80%
I'll leave you, baby.

112
00:06:43.415 --> 00:06:46.415 line:80%
The rest of the morning continues with cleaning up the kids.

113
00:06:46.415 --> 00:06:49.415 line:80%
Okay, let's get you cleaned up.

114
00:06:49.415 --> 00:06:53.415 line:80%
How was get you pronounced?

115
00:06:53.415 --> 00:06:57.415 line:80%
Have you noticed that it's really common to hear a CH in this phrase?

116
00:06:57.415 --> 00:06:59.415 line:80%
Let's get you cleaned up.

117
00:06:59.415 --> 00:07:03.415 line:80%
When a word that ends in a T is followed by you or your,

118
00:07:03.415 --> 00:07:07.915 line:80%
That T often turns into a CH. Chew, chew, get you.

119
00:07:07.915 --> 00:07:10.355 line:80%
There's no rule about doing this or not.

120
00:07:10.355 --> 00:07:14.915 line:80%
It's just a habit that happens and many Americans do it a lot of the time.

121
00:07:14.915 --> 00:07:18.855 line:80%
If you don't do it, you can do a stop T. Get you, get you.

122
00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:20.855 line:80%
Let's get you cleaned up.

123
00:07:20.855 --> 00:07:32.783 line:80%
Yum Any other flavors available today Stony you want these waffle pretzels for your snack right

124
00:07:35.063 --> 00:07:39.963 line:80%
Do you want one right now?

125
00:07:44.143 --> 00:07:45.083 line:80%
Special treat.

126
00:07:47.003 --> 00:07:50.443 line:80%
Snack. We all need a good snack every once in a while.

127
00:07:50.443 --> 00:07:55.423 line:80%
This is not a full meal, but a little bit of food that we eat between meals.

128
00:07:55.423 --> 00:07:56.923 line:80%
Snack.

129
00:07:56.923 --> 00:08:00.763 line:80%
At our house, we get up anywhere between 6 and 6.45.

130
00:08:00.763 --> 00:08:05.623 line:80%
Stoney doesn't leave for school until after 9, so we have a lot of time to fill up.

131
00:08:05.623 --> 00:08:09.483 line:80%
Sometimes we go out for a walk, but often we just play at home.

132
00:08:14.043 --> 00:08:16.043 line:80%
I've already got it all ready for ya.

133
00:08:16.043 --> 00:08:18.043 line:80%
Did you hear the U reduction?

134
00:08:18.043 --> 00:08:20.043 line:80%
Ya. Ya.

135
00:08:20.043 --> 00:08:22.043 line:80%
I've already got it all ready for you.

136
00:08:22.043 --> 00:08:27.043 line:80%
Hey, hey, we're not skipping teeth. Stand up.

137
00:08:27.043 --> 00:08:29.043 line:80%
Stoney.

138
00:08:29.043 --> 00:08:34.043 line:80%
We're not skipping teeth. I'm sure many parents can relate to this.

139
00:08:34.043 --> 00:08:38.043 line:80%
When you skip something, you don't do something that is part of a regular sequence.

140
00:08:38.043 --> 00:08:43.043 line:80%
In this case, brushing teeth in the morning is definitely part of our morning routine.

141
00:08:43.043 --> 00:08:45.043 line:80%
Stand up.

142
00:08:45.043 --> 00:08:48.043 line:80%
Mercy.

143
00:08:48.043 --> 00:08:55.163 line:80%
Mercy. This is an exclamation of exasperation, surprise, anger, or frustration.

144
00:08:55.163 --> 00:09:00.443 line:80%
I was feeling all of those things. Luckily, he did decide to brush his teeth.

145
00:09:00.443 --> 00:09:03.243 line:80%
And now we brush teeth. It's just part of getting ready for school.

146
00:09:05.883 --> 00:09:10.043 line:80%
My camera died. After that, we got Stoney dressed and David took him to school.

147
00:09:10.043 --> 00:09:15.083 line:80%
Then he came home and took Sawyer while I went to work. And that is our morning routine.

148
00:09:15.083 --> 00:09:19.963 line:80%
I hope you liked this video. I hope you learned a little something. Please subscribe. I make

149
00:09:19.963 --> 00:09:25.803 line:80%
new videos every Tuesday. Did you see anything that surprised you or learned anything new?

150
00:09:25.803 --> 00:09:30.603 line:80%
Put it in the comments below. I love to hear what you're learning with the videos.

151
00:09:30.603 --> 00:09:34.283 line:80%
That's it, and thanks so much for using Rachel's English.

152
